[%emt 0:00:36] [%think 2872] [%out 3172] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0]
[%cachewrite 1421] Black's knight on d5 attacks my knight on c3. The black
bishop on e7 and my bishop on h4 also attack each other. I think the best move
is to take on d5 first. - **Recapturing with a pawn loses a piece:** if Black
plays exd5 or cxd5, then Bxe7 wins the bishop, and I am a full piece up. -
**Pinning the rook also loses a piece:** if Black plays Bg5 against the rook on
d2, then Bxg5 hxg5. My knight on d5 is still hanging, so it retreats safely,
and I am a piece up. - **Black's best is Bxh4:** then Ne7+. After Kh7 or Kh8,
Nxc6 wins a pawn, and my knight can later drop back to d4. Taking on e7 right
away with Bxe7 Nxe7 only keeps material equal. So I take on d5. } 15... Bxh4
16. Ne7+ { [%emt 0:00:48] [%think 4116] [%out 4310] [%in 2] [%cacheread 0]
[%cachewrite 1445] The d5 knight is attacked by both the c6 and e6 pawns. Black
is also threatening Bg5: the bishop would pin my d2 rook against the king on
c1, and then take it to win the exchange. Ne7+ is check, so it gains a tempo.
After Black's king moves, I play Nxc8 and trade off the c8 bishop. Once my rook
leaves d2, the Bg5 pin threat goes away. The resulting endgame is good for me:
my rooks can go to d6 or d7, and Black's c6 and a6 pawns are weak. } 16... Bxe7
